1.2  How the kernel shares the CPUs

TL;DR
  - There are more tasks wanting a CPU than CPUs, so the scheduler gives each a
    short turn, then switches: a context switch.
  - Linux preempts: it can take a CPU back from a task at any moment, so that
    nothing hogs the machine.
  - Since kernel 6.6 the policy is EEVDF: tasks get CPU time in proportion to
    their weight (their niceness, 16.3).

      vmstat 1                      # cs: switches per second, r: tasks waiting
      grep ctxt /proc/self/status   # switches of one process

On your machine
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64, built with gcc 14.2.0
  Build     #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Debian 6.12.48-1 (2025-09-20)
  Work      in the 3 days, 4 h since boot: 912.3 million context switches (3,330
            per second), 1.2 million processes started, 51.2 million interrupts
  Now       3 tasks running or ready, 1 waiting on I/O
  Disk FS   ext4, vfat, btrfs

Pitfalls
  - Thousands of context switches per second are normal. A sudden jump often
    means threads fighting over a lock, or waking up too often.
  - Voluntary switches (the task waits for something) are harmless; many
    involuntary ones mean the task wanted more CPU than it got.
  - More threads than CPUs doesn't make CPU-bound work faster: they only take
    turns.
  - In a VM, st (steal) in top is CPU time the host gave to someone else while
    your task was ready.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 1.2 --why

Related
